{"title": "Fractions Revision: Equivalent Fractions and Comparing Them", "intro": "Tonight we will help your child understand how fractions can look different but mean the same amount, and how to tell which fraction is bigger when they have different denominators.", "sections": [{"heading": "What Are Equivalent Fractions?", "points": ["Equivalent fractions are different fractions that show the same amount shaded.", "When you cut the parts smaller and take more of them, you can make an equivalent fraction.", "The whole bar or shape must stay the same size to compare them fairly.", "Both the numerator (top number) and denominator (bottom number) must change together."], "example": "If 1/2 of a bar is shaded, you can split each part in half to get 2/4 shaded. You cut 2 parts into 4 parts, and shade 2 of them instead of 1. The amount shaded is the same."}, {"heading": "Finding an Equivalent Fraction", "points": ["Look at the denominator: how many equal parts is the whole cut into?", "Look at the numerator: how many parts are shaded?", "To make a new equivalent fraction, multiply both numbers by the same amount.", "Count on the bar model: if you split each part in two, you have twice as many parts shaded and twice as many parts total."], "example": "To find an equivalent fraction for 3/5 with denominator 10: multiply 3 and 5 both by 2. You get 6/10. On a bar: the 5 parts become 10 smaller parts, and the 3 shaded parts become 6 shaded parts."}, {"heading": "Comparing Fractions with Different Denominators", "points": ["Draw both fractions as bar models, using the same size bar.", "Look at how much of each bar is shaded, not the numbers.", "When fractions have the same numerator, a bigger denominator means smaller pieces, so the fraction is smaller.", "List fractions from smallest to largest (ascending order) or largest to smallest (descending order) by looking at the shaded area."], "example": "Compare 1/2 and 1/8. Draw two same-size bars. Cut one into 2 parts and shade 1. Cut the other into 8 parts and shade 1. The first bar has much more shaded. So 1/2 is greater than 1/8. When the top number stays the same, more pieces means smaller pieces."}, {"heading": "Checking Your Work", "points": ["Ask: Do both bars show the same size whole?", "Ask: Did I multiply (or divide) both the top and bottom by the same number?", "Ask: Does the shaded area look the same on both bar models?", "Ask: Did I compare the shaded amount, not just the bottom numbers?"], "example": "Your child says 1/3 is bigger than 1/2. Draw the bars the same size and ask: which shaded part looks bigger? The 1/2 is clearly bigger. Then ask: why did the bigger denominator (3) make the fraction smaller? Because the pieces got smaller."}], "remember": ["Always draw bar models the same physical size to compare them fairly.", "Change both the numerator and denominator together when making equivalent fractions, never just one.", "When the top number is the same, a bigger bottom number means smaller pieces and a smaller fraction.", "Use the bar model picture to decide which is bigger, not just the numbers."]}
```
